UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 14, Lesson 2: Rehearsing a class play', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Rehearsing a class play uses blocking (planned moves), cues (lines or signals that trigger action) and written notes. Run scenes slowly, then at pace. Actors hold scripts until lines are secure, then go off-book. Stay quiet when not on, and write director notes kindly.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA cue is missed because two actors speak over each other. Give two rehearsal fixes.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Mark whose line ends the cue in the script; practise the handoff three times slowly.'' Clear ownership of cues.\n\nWATCH FOR\nChanging blocking every run without writing it down. Consistency needs recorded decisions.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nRehearse one scene with a blocking sketch, three cue highlights and a notes column. Perform once and list two improvements.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"Rehearsing a class play uses blocking (planned moves), cues (lines or signals that trigger action) and written notes. Run scenes slowly, then at pace. Actors hold scripts until lines are secure, then go off-book. Stay quiet when not on, and write director notes kindly.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Create and perform","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-DRA-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Rehearse one scene with a blocking sketch, three cue highlights and a notes column. Perform once and list two improvements. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using rehearsal on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Changing blocking every run without writing it down.. Correction: Strong answer: ''Mark whose line ends the cue in the script;. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Changing blocking every run without writing it down. Consistency needs recorded decisions. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating rehearsal on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with rehearsal, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about rehearsal with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for rehearsal. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for rehearsal. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson.","''Mark whose line ends the cue in the script;","Changing blocking every run without writing it down.","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material."],"question":"A cue is missed because two actors speak over each other. Give two rehearsal fixes. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for rehearsal; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["''Mark whose line ends the cue in the script;","Careful use of blocking and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Changing blocking every run without writing it down.","Rehearsing a class play uses blocking (planned moves), cues (lines or signals that trigger action) and written notes."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with rehearsal?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named blocking and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply rehearsal to new material and name the blocking and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Changing blocking every run without writing it down.","Name blocking and explain why it matters only, without linking it to rehearsal."],"question":"Which response best applies rehearsal with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on rehearsal. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Changing blocking every run without writing it down.","''Mark whose line ends the cue in the script;"],"question":"Which response best uses elements, techniques and audience effect for rehearsal?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Changing blocking every run without writing it down.","Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for rehearsal."],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about rehearsal?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Example: Blocking note: ''Enter left on bell sound;","Changing blocking every run without writing it down.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Rehearsing a class play uses blocking (planned moves), cues (lines or signals that trigger action) — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue."],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for rehearsal?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name blocking and explain why it matters only, with no linked example.","Rehearse one scene with a blocking sketch, three cue highlights and a notes column.","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Changing blocking every run without writing it down."],"question":"Which independent response best applies rehearsal to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about rehearsal with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating rehearsal on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using rehearsal on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for rehearsal. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with rehearsal, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Changing blocking every run without writing it down.. Correction: Strong answer: ''Mark whose line ends the cue in the script;. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y4-w14-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise rehearsal.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Drama","success_criteria":["I can use rehearsal with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify blocking and explain why it matters.","I can practise cue independently on a new prompt.","I can notice notes needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Rehearsing a class play uses blocking (planned moves), cues (lines or signals that trigger action) and written notes.","options":["Rehearsing a class play uses blocking (planned moves), cues (lines or signals that trigger action) and written notes.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about rehearsal with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Blocking note: ''Enter left on bell sound; sit centre on line three.'' After a messy cross, they mark an X on the floor plan and practise the path twice.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA cue is missed because two actors speak over each other. Give two rehearsal fixes.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Mark whose line ends the cue in the script; practise the handoff three times slowly.'' Clear ownership of cues.","explain":"Rehearsing a class play uses blocking (planned moves), cues (lines or signals that trigger action) and written notes. Run scenes slowly, then at pace. Actors hold scripts until lines are secure, then go off-book. Stay quiet when not on, and write director notes kindly.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing blocking every run without writing it down. Consistency needs recorded decisions. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Rehearsal tools.","caption":"Write it down.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Blocking</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">planned paths</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Cue</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">trigger line/sound</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Blocking note: ''Enter left on bell sound; sit centre on line three.'' After a messy cross, they mark an X on the Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Blocking note: ''Enter left on bell sound; sit centre on line three.'' After a messy cross, they mark an X on the Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about rehearsal with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Blocking note: ''Enter left on bell sound; sit centre on line three.'' After a messy cross, they mark an X on the floor plan and practise the path twice. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Rehearsal order.","caption":"Slow then up.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Slow run</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">fix handoffs</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Then pace</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">performance speed</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about rehearsal with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nChanging blocking every run without writing it down. Consistency needs recorded decisions. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Blocking note: ''Enter left on bell sound; sit centre on line three.'' After a messy cross, they mark an X on the floor plan and practise the path twice.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Mark whose line ends the cue in the script; practise the handoff three times slowly.'' Clear ownership of cues.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Changing blocking every run without writing it down. Consistency needs recorded decisions. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example.","A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about rehearsal with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nRehearsing a class play uses blocking (planned moves), cues (lines or signals that trigger action) and written notes. Run scenes slowly, then at pace. Actors hold scripts until lines are secure, then go off-book. Stay quiet when not on, and write director notes kindly. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 14, Lesson 2: Rehearsing a class play","unit_number":3,"unit_title":"Improvisation and playbuilding","week":14,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Blocking note: ''Enter left on bell sound; sit centre on line three.'' After a messy cross, they mark an X on the floor plan and practise the path twice.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Rehearsal tools.","caption":"Write it down.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Blocking</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">planned paths</text